---

### Summary of behavioral additions

| Flag | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| `-r` / `--recursive` | Directory sources are copied recursively; symlinks (operand or within tree) are recreated as symlinks without following; self/descendant copy is rejected before any writes |
| `-f` with recursive | Force still allows removing a non-directory dest that cannot be opened, then creating a replacement |
| `-i` with recursive | Interactive prompt fires before overwriting an existing regular-file or symlink destination; decline preserves dest and contributes exit status 1 |

### Interpretations chosen

- "Destination symlink collisions within a copied tree are excluded" → the test suite will not contain such cases; the implementation simply overwrites any existing dest (file or symlink) when recreating a symlink in recursive mode.
- "An initial empty output directory may remain" → my implementation checks self-copy before creating the dest directory, so no empty directory is left behind (strictly stronger).
- "Resource limits must produce a diagnostic rather than looping or crashing" → all I/O errors are captured as `io::Error` and returned as `CopyResult::Failed`; no unbounded retry loops exist.
